-- #1111 A-2.13 (#1319 unit 3) — #1376's VALUE-ONLY shape, with the collision made
-- OBSERVABLE.  This is the cell the round-2 adversarial review of PR #1381 said
-- the unit's corpus could not express, and it is the SUCCESSOR to the assertion
-- `test/must_fail_fixtures/1376-value-only-import-record-floor/claim.txt` carried
-- before that pin was deleted:
--
--   "a same-FIELD-TYPE variant has NOT been probed and could be silently wrong;
--    if one is found, re-grade #1376 to S0."
--
-- 🚨 ONE WAS FOUND.  It is #1382 — same-name records at opposite field orders
-- corrupting each other's layout in the built binary, `check` 0, `run` correct,
-- binary silently wrong, segfault on type-divergent fields.  #1382 was fixed on
-- `main` by PR #1395 (`958bf53c`), which narrowed `lookupRecordByMangledHead`'s
-- give-up arm by the receiver's own `HeadKey` instead of letting
-- `pairRecordByName (headL owners)` decide a projection by module-name SORT ORDER.
-- Unit 3 is rebased onto that fix, and this cell is the assertion turned into a
-- gate rather than retired by deletion.
--
-- 🚨 WHICH MECHANISM EACH HALF OF THIS CELL ACTUALLY GRADES — DERIVED FROM THE
-- TYPED CORE IR, NOT ASSERTED.  An earlier version of this header implied the
-- whole cell grades #1319 unit 3.  It does not, and the dump says so plainly.
-- `compiler/entries/core_ir_typed_modules_dump_main.mdk` over this fixture prints:
--
--   CFieldAccess (CVar "armod__mkA" AGlobal) "a" "armod__Cfg"
--   CFieldAccess (CVar "armod__mkA" AGlobal) "b" "armod__Cfg"
--   CFieldAccess (CVar "zrmod__mkZ" AGlobal) "a" "zrmod__Cfg"
--   CFieldAccess (CVar "zrmod__mkZ" AGlobal) "b" "zrmod__Cfg"
--
-- Every stamp is MANGLED.  Unit 3's arm (a) returns `Some (r, ri)` where `r` is
-- `headTyconNameMono te` — the UNMANGLED head name `Cfg` — so it cannot produce
-- `armod__Cfg` at all.  These stamps came from `lookupRecordByMangledHead`, i.e.
-- from PR #1395's receiver-identity narrowing, and they are correct: each receiver
-- carries its own module's record.  Therefore:
--
--   * the LAYOUT half of this cell RE-GRADES #1395, not unit 3;
--   * the ACCEPTANCE half grades unit 3 — `main` (with unit 3's selection stubbed
--     back out) REFUSES this program at exit 1, `Type mismatch: Cfg vs Cfg — …
--     'armod' … 'zrmod'` (#1376), measured, so the program only exists to be
--     layout-graded because unit 3 accepts it.
--
-- Both halves are worth keeping in one cell: the acceptance is unit 3's and the
-- layout is #1395's, and a regression in EITHER reds this row.  Do not try to build
-- a cell that grades unit 3's layout half — on the emit path unit 3's arm cannot
-- produce a stamp at all, so there may be no such cell to build.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY THE EXISTING #1382 CELLS DO NOT COVER THIS.  `record_xmod_field_order/`
-- and its siblings project INSIDE the declaring module and their entry imports
-- plain `Int`s; this one projects in `entry`, on a value reached ONLY through an
-- imported binding, which is the arm unit 3 newly enables.
--
-- ⚠️ EVAL IS A KNOWN-WRONG ORACLE HERE, IN THE REASSURING DIRECTION.  The
-- interpreter resolves fields by NAME at run time (`evalField (VRecord _ fields)`,
-- `compiler/eval/eval.mdk`), so it is structurally immune to the layout question
-- and prints the right answer even when the binary does not.  The golden below was
-- NOT taken from `medaka run`; it is hand-derived, and the cell is graded on the
-- EXECUTED NATIVE (and WasmGC) BINARY by `diff_compiler_engines.sh`'s module arm.
--
-- Hand-derived from the sources, before any engine was run:
--   armod: mkA = Cfg { a = 11, b = 22 }  (order a,b)  =>  mkA.a = 11, mkA.b = 22
--   zrmod: mkZ = Cfg { b = 33, a = 44 }  (order b,a)  =>  mkZ.a = 44, mkZ.b = 33
-- so `11,22,44,33`.  A wrong pick for `mkZ` prints `11,22,33,44`.
import armod.{mkA}
import zrmod.{mkZ}

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  putStrLn
    (intToString mkA.a
      ++ ","
      ++ intToString mkA.b
      ++ ","
      ++ intToString mkZ.a
      ++ ","
      ++ intToString mkZ.b)
